**Clause B is broader in scope and more favorable to the indemnitee.**

### Why Clause B is broader
Clause B:
- covers **“any and all claims, losses, damages, and expenses”** — a wider category of recoverable items than Clause A’s “losses” only;
- includes a duty to **indemnify, defend, and hold harmless** — adding an affirmative **defense obligation**, which is typically very valuable to the indemnitee;
- applies to claims **“arising from the performance of this agreement”** — a broader trigger than Clause A’s narrower focus on losses arising only from a party’s own negligence or willful misconduct;
- applies **“regardless of fault”** — meaning the indemnitee can recover even when fault is not established against the indemnitor;
- excludes liability only for losses caused by the other party’s **gross negligence or intentional misconduct**, which is a narrower carve-out than Clause A’s limitation.

### Why Clause A is narrower
Clause A is limited to:
- losses arising out of a party’s **own negligence or willful misconduct**;
- no express duty to defend;
- no express “hold harmless” language;
- no coverage for broader contract-related claims or expenses.

### Bottom line
**Clause B is the broader, more indemnity-friendly clause** because it expands the types of covered losses, adds defense and hold harmless obligations, and applies more generally to agreement-related claims.

